This is a well-known cross-browser compatibility issue that many developers encounter. Firefox has stricter security policies around the window.opener object compared to Chrome. This is a deliberate security decision made by the Firefox development team to protect users from potential security vulnerabilities. Understanding why this happens is important before we look at solutions.

Firefox restricts access to window.opener primarily for security reasons, especially in cross-origin scenarios. Even in same-origin scenarios, Firefox may limit what properties of window.opener are accessible. This behavior differs significantly from Chrome, which is more permissive in this regard.

Approach 1: Store and pass the parent window name via URL parameters. Before opening the child window, make sure the parent window has a name set. Then pass that name as a URL query parameter when opening the child window. This is one of the most reliable cross-browser solutions available.

Here is the code for the parent window:
window.name = 'parentTab';
window.open('someUrl?parentName=parentTab', '_blank');

Here is the code for the child window:
const params = new URLSearchParams(window.location.search);
const parentName = params.get('parentName');
function switchToParent() {
  window.open('', parentName);
}

Approach 2: Use the BroadcastChannel API for inter-tab communication. The BroadcastChannel API is a modern and reliable way to communicate between tabs of the same origin. It is well-supported in Firefox and other modern browsers.

Here is the code for the parent window using BroadcastChannel:
const channel = new BroadcastChannel('tab_channel');
channel.onmessage = function(event) {
  if (event.data === 'focusParent') {
    window.focus();
  }
};

Here is the code for the child window using BroadcastChannel:
const channel = new BroadcastChannel('tab_channel');
function switchToParent() {
  channel.postMessage('focusParent');
}

Approach 3: Use localStorage or sessionStorage to share the parent window name between tabs. Before opening the child window, store the parent window name in localStorage. In the child window, read this value and use it to navigate back to the parent.

Here is the code for the parent window using localStorage:
