localStorage.setItem('parentWindowName', 'parentTab');
window.open('someUrl', '_blank');

Here is the code for the child window using localStorage:
const parentName = localStorage.getItem('parentWindowName');
function switchToParent() {
  window.open('', parentName);
}

Approach 4: Use the window.postMessage API. This is a secure way to communicate between windows regardless of browser restrictions. You can send a message from the child window to the parent window asking it to focus itself.

Approach 5: Wrap window.opener access in a try-catch block. Even though Firefox restricts window.opener, you can try wrapping the access in a try-catch block to handle the error gracefully and fall back to another method if needed.

Here is an example of the try-catch approach:
try {
  if (window.opener && window.opener.name) {
    window.open('', window.opener.name);
  }
} catch(e) {
  // Fall back to stored parent name
  const parentName = localStorage.getItem('parentWindowName');
  window.open('', parentName);
}

Important note about window.focus(): Even after successfully referencing the parent window, calling window.focus() or switching tabs programmatically may be restricted by browsers including Firefox. Most modern browsers require a user gesture to allow programmatic focus switching between tabs to prevent unwanted behavior.

The most recommended and reliable cross-browser solution is the combination of Approach 1 (passing parent window name via URL parameters) and Approach 2 (using BroadcastChannel API). These two approaches together will cover most use cases and work reliably across Chrome, Firefox, and other modern browsers.

